 1 big thing: Fresh inflation warning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data : Bureau of Labor Statistics via FRED. Chart: Emily Peck/Axios Prices for one of the world's least glamorous products — bare printed circuit boards — are skyrocketing to record levels, Axios' Emily Peck reports. 

 That's a blazing-loud warning for more inflation to come. 🟩 The big picture: Bare printed circuit boards are the green rectangles that house silicon chips.

 They're in everything from smartphones and cars to medical and military equipment. 🤖 Data centers and AI labs are fueling huge price increases for computer chips — but also the simple boards that they get placed on.

 The Producer Price Index for the boards rose 45% in July from the same time last year. That reflects prices charged by domestic manufacturers. The U.S. produces only about 4% of the global supply, but the data still illustrates the pricing squeeze. 📈 Between the lines: The Iran war has pushed up prices for the boards' basic components: primarily resin and copper.

 A major manufacturer of resin in Saudi Arabia was knocked offline in April after an Iranian attack. That one plant makes 70% of the world's high-purity polyphenylene ether resin. 👀 What we're watching: Price hikes tied to higher circuit board costs will take a while to hit your wallet.

 Jason Miller, a professor of supply chain management at Michigan State University's business school, tells Axios: "It takes at least six months, if not a year, for these price increases fairly far upstream in the supply chain to start to make their way to end users." Go deeper . 

 
 
